rtg.library/AddBoardMode                              rtg.library/AddBoardMode

   NAME
	AddBoardMode -- Adds a mode to a board's list.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddBoardMode(rb: *RtgBase, board: *rtg.RtgBoard, timings: *const rtg.RtgTimings, format: u32, flags: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.19. LVO -384.

   INPUTS
	- `board` - the board, one of the caller's own.
	- `timings` - the mode's timings: its picture's size, and where the
	  display has a clock, the clock and the blanking. Copied.
	- `format` - the pixel format the board shows the mode in, as a number.
	- `flags` - `RTGMF_DEFAULT` for the mode the board comes up in,
	  `RTGMF_TURNED` for a picture that lies on the panel a quarter turn
	  round.

   RESULT
	The mode's number, from 1; 0 for timings whose picture is empty, for a
	format this build does not know, and without memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	The library makes the mode: its name is its size ("1024x600") - with
	the refresh behind it where the board has that size at more than one -
	its refresh is what the timings come to, 0 where they have no clock,
	and its number is the next the board has not used. The list is kept
	with the larger picture first. The first mode added is the board's
	default until one is added with `RTGMF_DEFAULT`. A mode added again -
	the same size, format, refresh and turn - is the one that is there: it
	takes the timings handed over and keeps its number.

	On a board with a connector (`detect`) the modes are what the monitor
	names, made by the library; the ones added with this call are the
	board's own, which stand while no monitor is there or the one there
	names none the board can make.

	A row of a buffer of the mode is as long as `AllocBitMap` makes one:
	the width in the format, rounded up to what the board's display memory
	is aligned to. It is set once the board has joined the list, so a
	driver that needs the figure in its `attach` works it out from its own
	region.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: while another task holds the board's lock.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: takes the board's lock. No spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Task will do. A driver calls it in its `attach`.

   OWNERSHIP
	The mode is the library's and goes with the board. Nothing is kept of
	`timings`.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`ReadTimingTags`, `GetBoardModes`, `CreateBoard`

   EXAMPLES
	    var timings: rtg.RtgTimings = .{};
	    if (!rb.ReadTimingTags(tag_list, &timings)) return rtg.errors.RTGERR_BAD_TAGS;
	    _ = rb.AddBoardMode(board, &timings, @intFromEnum(rtg.PixelFormat.rgb565), rtg.modes.RTGMF_DEFAULT);
rtg.library/AddRtgDevice                              rtg.library/AddRtgDevice

   NAME
	AddRtgDevice -- Makes a device from tags and offers it to the drivers.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddRtgDevice(rb: *RtgBase, tag_list: ?[*]const TagItem) ?*rtg.RtgDevice

   SINCE
	1.18. LVO -368.

   INPUTS
	- `tag_list` - what the device is, in the tags a panel part of the
	  board's list has: `PART_Chip`, `PART_Bus`, `PART_ChipName`, and the
	  `RTGA_` tags its driver reads. Read for as long as the device lives.

   RESULT
	The device, or null without memory. It is answered whether a driver
	took it or not: `device.driver` says.

   BEHAVIOR
	For hardware the library cannot find by itself - a display that is
	neither a panel of the board's list nor a function on PCI, and the
	tests' own. The device joins the list as one of the part kind, named
	`panel.` and the next number, and is offered at once as
	`BindRtgDrivers` offers one: to the drivers whose table names it, the
	closest first. The boards its driver makes join the library's list.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes. A driver brings its hardware up in its `attach`.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: takes the device list's lock, and inside it the driver
	  list's and the board list's. No spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The device is the library's until `RemRtgDevice`. The tags stay the
	caller's and are kept valid until then.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`RemRtgDevice`, `BindRtgDrivers`, `CreateBoard`

   EXAMPLES
	    const device = rb.AddRtgDevice(&found_tags) orelse return error.NoMemory;
rtg.library/AddRtgDriver                              rtg.library/AddRtgDriver

   NAME
	AddRtgDriver -- Puts a driver's description on the library's list.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddRtgDriver(rb: *RtgBase, spec: *const rtg.RtgDriverSpec, seg_list: ?*anyopaque) ?*rtg.RtgDriver

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -332.

   INPUTS
	- `spec` - the driver's description: its name, what it makes and the
	  operations the library calls. It stays where it is and is read there
	  for as long as the driver is on the list.
	- `seg_list` - the segments of a driver that was loaded from a file,
	  kept in the node; null for one in ROM.

   RESULT
	The driver's node, or null: for operations that fill in neither
	`attach` nor `create_transport`, for a name already on the list, and
	without memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	The library makes the node and, in the same block behind it, the
	`state_size` bytes the description asks for, cleared; `state` is null
	for a driver that asks for none. The node carries what a driver needs
	to work: exec, this library as a driver calls it, and
	expansion.library where the machine has it. The block is in internal
	memory for a driver whose interrupts read it
	(`RTGDF_INTERNAL_INSTANCE`). The list is kept by the descriptions'
	priority, so of two that name a device as closely the more wanted is
	asked first. A driver that can take no device and make no bus would
	sit on the list answering nothing, so it is refused.

	The library calls this itself for every driver in the ROM, at its
	init; a test calls it for its own driver. No device is offered to the
	driver by this call: `BindRtgDrivers` offers the ones no driver has.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, while another task holds the driver list.
	- Interrupts: no. It may wait.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held: it waits for a lock.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The node and the state are the library's, freed by `RemRtgDriver`. The
	description stays its module's.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`RemRtgDriver`, `BindRtgDrivers`, `GetRtgDrivers`

   EXAMPLES
	    const driver = rb.AddRtgDriver(&my_spec, null) orelse return error.DriverRefused;
rtg.library/CloseTransport                          rtg.library/CloseTransport

   NAME
	CloseTransport -- Gives a bus back.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CloseTransport(rb: *RtgBase, io: ?*rtg.RtgTransport) void

   SINCE
	1.18. LVO -380.

   INPUTS
	- `io` - the bus, as `OpenTransport` answered it. Null does nothing.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The bus's driver's `destroy` is called, the bus leaves the library's
	list, and its handle and instance are freed. Whoever opened it sends
	nothing over it any more: a board's driver closes its bus when its
	bring-up fails, and from its board's `destroy`.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, while another task holds the board list, and if the
	  bus's driver does.
	- Interrupts: no. The driver may wait on its bus.
	- Locks: takes the board list's lock. No spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The bus is gone; its driver is one bus less.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenTransport`

   EXAMPLES
	    rb.CloseTransport(io);
rtg.library/CreateBoard                                rtg.library/CreateBoard

   NAME
	CreateBoard -- Makes a board for one output of a device.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CreateBoard(rb: *RtgBase, driver: *rtg.RtgDriver, device: *rtg.RtgDevice, spec: *const rtg.RtgBoardSpec) ?*rtg.RtgBoard

   SINCE
	1.18. LVO -360.

   INPUTS
	- `driver` - the driver that makes it: the one whose `attach` is
	  running for `device`.
	- `device` - the device the board is an output of.
	- `spec` - what the driver says of the board: its ops, what the output
	  is called, how many bytes of its own it keeps with the board, what
	  is true of it from the start, where its buffers come from, and which
	  output of the device it is; for a board with a connector, the pixel
	  format its monitor's modes are made in. Read during the call and not
	  kept; the label's string stays the driver's.

   RESULT
	The board, or null: when `driver` is not being offered `device`, and
	without memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	The board's handle and the driver's instance come from memory the
	driver can read from its interrupts (`RTGDF_INTERNAL_INSTANCE`), the
	instance cleared. The handle has the driver, the device, the ops, the
	region and the flags of the description; the driver fills in the
	rest - its modes (`AddBoardMode`), its info, the region where it was
	not known - before its `attach` answers. The board is on no list yet and has no name: it
	joins the library's list once the `attach` has answered `RTGERR_OK`,
	named after its driver and a number in the order the driver's boards
	were made ("rgb.0"), the firmware's picture last on the list. A board
	comes up in no mode and showing nothing; `ShowBoard` is what puts a
	picture up, and what its driver lights the output with.

	An `attach` that fails after making a board deletes it (`DeleteBoard`);
	one it leaves is freed by the library.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: called inside `attach`, with the device list's and the driver
	  list's locks held by the caller's task.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The board is the library's from when it joins the list, and is never
	freed for a program; until then it is the driver's to delete. The
	driver is one board more.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`DeleteBoard`, `AddBoardMode`, `BindRtgDrivers`, `AddRtgDevice`,
	`NextBoard`

   EXAMPLES
	    const board = rb.CreateBoard(driver, device, &.{ .ops = &ops, .instance_size = @sizeOf(Panel) }) orelse return rtg.errors.RTGERR_NO_MEMORY;
rtg.library/DeleteBoard                                rtg.library/DeleteBoard

   NAME
	DeleteBoard -- Gives a board back: stops it and frees everything it held.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn DeleteBoard(rb: *RtgBase, board: ?*rtg.RtgBoard) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -364.

   INPUTS
	- `board` - the board, as `CreateBoard` answered it. Null does nothing.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	A driver's call, for two cases. An `attach` that fails after it made a
	board deletes it: the board has not joined the list, the driver has
	given back what it took for it, and the handle and the instance are
	freed - its `destroy` is not called. And a driver that is being removed
	deletes its boards, which only a test's driver is: a program is never
	handed a board that goes away.

	A board on the list that a task still waits for a frame of
	(`WaitVBlank`) is refused - it stays on the list, which is how a
	caller sees it - and nothing of it is touched: the waiter would be
	left linked into a list that is freed. So is one with a video layer
	still open (`CloseVideoLayer` first). Otherwise it is taken off the display first, then every buffer
	it still has is freed, the driver's `destroy` is called, and the board
	leaves the list and its device. The driver is one board less.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, while another task holds the board list, and if the
	  driver does.
	- Interrupts: no. The driver may wait on its bus.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held: a driver may wait.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The board, its buffers and its display memory are gone.

   BUGS
	The answer of taking the board off the display is not looked at: one
	whose driver refuses to show nothing has its buffers freed and its
	driver's `destroy` called all the same.

   SEE ALSO
	`CreateBoard`, `RemRtgDevice`, `RemRtgDriver`

   EXAMPLES
	    rb.DeleteBoard(board);
rtg.library/FindStandardTimings                rtg.library/FindStandardTimings

   NAME
	FindStandardTimings -- Looks up the timings a standard gives a picture of a size at a refresh.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FindStandardTimings(_: *RtgBase, width: u32, height: u32, refresh_hz: u32, timings: *rtg.RtgTimings) bool

   SINCE
	1.20. LVO -396.

   INPUTS
	- `width` - the picture's width in pixels.
	- `height` - its height in lines.
	- `refresh_hz` - frames a second, to the nearest: 60 for 59.94.
	- `timings` - where the timings go.

   RESULT
	True, and `timings` filled in. False, and `timings` left as it was,
	for a size at a refresh the library has no standard timing of.

   BEHAVIOR
	The timings are those a monitor means when it names a mode without
	its timing: CEA-861's for the modes it numbers - the number is in
	`vic` - and VESA's for the sizes it times, each with its pixel clock,
	porches, sync pulses and their polarity. It is the table the library
	makes a monitor's modes of, so a driver that measured a picture, or
	has one size to bring up without a monitor's word, has the standard's
	figures for it.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: safe: it reads a constant table.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing is allocated.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddBoardMode`, `ReadTimingTags`

   EXAMPLES
	    var timings: rtg.RtgTimings = .{};
	    if (!rb.FindStandardTimings(1920, 1080, 60, &timings)) return rtg.errors.RTGERR_BAD_MODE;
rtg.library/FrameDone                                    rtg.library/FrameDone

   NAME
	FrameDone -- Says that a frame of a board's display ended.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FrameDone(rb: *RtgBase, board: *rtg.RtgBoard) void

   SINCE
	1.22. LVO -340.

   INPUTS
	- `board` - the board whose display ended a frame: one of the
	  caller's own.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The frame is counted (`RtgBoardStats.blanks`), and every task that
	waits for frames of the board (`WaitVBlank`) is counted one frame on
	and signalled when its count is reached. From the first call on the
	board is one that says its frames (`RTGBC_VBLANK`): a wait is for
	them, with a limit, and no longer by the clock.

	A driver calls it once a frame, from the interrupt that says the
	frame ended - the blanking's, or the one that starts the next
	picture. A driver without such an interrupt does not call it, and
	its board is waited for by the clock.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: yes. That is where it is called.
	- Locks: none taken. The count and the walk of the waiters are inside
	  Disable, which an interrupt is in already.
	- Process: any, or none.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing is allocated.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`WaitVBlank`, `GetBoardStats`, `MonitorChanged`

   EXAMPLES
	    // The frame interrupt's server.
	    panel.rtg_base.FrameDone(panel.board);
rtg.library/GetRtgTagData                            rtg.library/GetRtgTagData

   NAME
	GetRtgTagData -- Returns a tag's data from a list, or a default.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GetRtgTagData(rb: *RtgBase, tag_value: Tag, default_value: usize, tag_list: ?[*]const TagItem) usize

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -356.

   INPUTS
	- `tag_value` - the tag.
	- `default_value` - what to answer when the list does not have it.
	- `tag_list` - the list.

   RESULT
	The first matching item's data, or `default_value`.

   BEHAVIOR
	utility.library's `GetTagData`, here so a driver need open nothing but
	this library.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: safe in itself: it reads the list.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing is allocated.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CreateBoard`, `AddRtgDevice`

   EXAMPLES
	    const hz = rb.GetRtgTagData(MY_Clock, 12_000_000, tags);
rtg.library/MonitorChanged                          rtg.library/MonitorChanged

   NAME
	MonitorChanged -- Has a board's connector looked at again.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn MonitorChanged(rb: *RtgBase, board: *rtg.RtgBoard) void

   SINCE
	1.20. LVO -392.

   INPUTS
	- `board` - the board at whose connector something happened: one of
	  the caller's own, whose ops fill in `detect`.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The board is marked and the library's monitor task woken; the call
	does no more. The task then asks the board's `detect`, keeps what the
	monitor now there says of itself, and makes the board's modes again
	of the timings it names that the board's `check_mode` takes - a mode
	of the figures of one the board had keeps that mode's number. A board
	that shows is handed the mode it is in and what it shows again
	(`show`), with the timings the new monitor gives it, where the new
	list has that mode; where it has not, the board shows nothing and is
	in no mode until it is shown in one. The board's `changes` are raised and the tasks that asked are
	told (`AddRtgNotify`).

	A driver calls it from its hot-plug interrupt, or from a task of its
	own that looks at the port. Several calls before the task has looked
	are one look, and a look that finds what the last one found - the same
	monitor, or none still - changes nothing: a driver whose connector has
	no interrupt may call it now and then. On a board without `detect` it
	does nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: yes: a flag is written and a task signalled.
	- Locks: none taken.
	- Process: any, or none.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing is allocated.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`GetBoardMonitor`, `AddRtgNotify`, `AddBoardMode`

   EXAMPLES
	    // The hot-plug line's interrupt.
	    rb.MonitorChanged(board);
rtg.library/OpenTransport                            rtg.library/OpenTransport

   NAME
	OpenTransport -- Opens the bus a device is reached over.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn OpenTransport(rb: *RtgBase, driver: *rtg.RtgDriver, device: *rtg.RtgDevice) ?*rtg.RtgTransport

   SINCE
	1.18. LVO -376.

   INPUTS
	- `driver` - the driver that asks: the one that has `device`, or is
	  being offered it.
	- `device` - the device on the bus.

   RESULT
	The bus, or null: when `driver` neither has the device nor is being
	offered it, for a bus no transport driver's table names, without
	memory, and when no transport driver could make it.

   BEHAVIOR
	A panel on a bus is two drivers: one for the panel, which takes the
	device, and one for the bus. The transport drivers whose table names
	the device - its bus, most often - are asked in turn, the closest
	first, to make the bus from the device's tags (`create_transport`):
	the pads, the clock and the framing are tags of the part. The first
	that answers `RTGERR_OK` has made it. The handle and the driver's
	instance come from memory the bus's driver can read from its
	interrupts. The panel's driver talks over the bus with `TxParam`,
	`TxColor` and `RxParam`.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, while another task holds the driver list or the board
	  list, and if the bus's driver does.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: takes the driver list's lock and the board list's. No
	  spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The bus is the caller's until `CloseTransport`. Its driver is one bus
	more.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CloseTransport`, `TxParam`, `TxColor`, `RxParam`

   EXAMPLES
	    const io = rb.OpenTransport(driver, device) orelse return rtg.errors.RTGERR_NO_DISPLAY;
rtg.library/ReadTimingTags                          rtg.library/ReadTimingTags

   NAME
	ReadTimingTags -- Reads the generic timing tags of a list into a structure.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ReadTimingTags(rb: *RtgBase, tag_list: ?[*]const TagItem, timings: *rtg.RtgTimings) bool

   SINCE
	1.19. LVO -388.

   INPUTS
	- `tag_list` - a device's tags.
	- `timings` - where the timings go.

   RESULT
	True, and `timings` filled in. False, and `timings` left as it was,
	for a list without `RTGA_Width` or `RTGA_Height`, and for one that
	names a pixel clock and lacks a sync pulse.

   BEHAVIOR
	`RTGA_Width` and `RTGA_Height` are the picture; `RTGA_PixelClock` the
	clock; `RTGA_HSyncPulse`, `RTGA_HBackPorch`, `RTGA_HFrontPorch` and
	the three for the vertical the blanking; `RTGA_TimingFlags` the
	`RTGTM_*` flags. A tag that is not there reads as 0. A list with no
	clock is a display that has none - a controller behind a bus - and
	needs no more than its size; one with a clock is a panel that is
	streamed its picture, and a panel without a sync pulse is not one.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: safe in itself: it reads the list.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing is allocated.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddBoardMode`, `GetRtgTagData`

   EXAMPLES
	    var timings: rtg.RtgTimings = .{};
	    if (!rb.ReadTimingTags(tag_list, &timings)) return rtg.errors.RTGERR_BAD_TAGS;
rtg.library/RemRtgDevice                              rtg.library/RemRtgDevice

   NAME
	RemRtgDevice -- Takes away a device `AddRtgDevice` made.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RemRtgDevice(rb: *RtgBase, device: ?*rtg.RtgDevice) void

   SINCE
	1.18. LVO -372.

   INPUTS
	- `device` - the device, as `AddRtgDevice` answered it. Null does
	  nothing, and so does one of the machine's own: a panel part and a
	  function on PCI never go.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	Every board its driver made of it is marked as gone first
	(`RTGBF_GONE`, `RTGBF_NO_DISPLAY`): taken off the display, passed over
	by `NextBoard` from now on, and answering `RTGERR_NO_DISPLAY` to a
	show - but still a handle, with its buffers, for whoever holds it.
	Then the driver's `detach` is called, where it has one, to stop the
	hardware, and the device leaves the list and is freed. The boards are
	the driver's to delete (`DeleteBoard`) when it is removed itself.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, while another task holds the device list or a board's
	  lock, and if the driver does.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: takes the device list's lock, and inside it each board's. No
	  spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The device is gone, and its tags are the caller's again. Its boards
	stay the library's. The driver is one device less.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddRtgDevice`, `DeleteBoard`, `NextBoard`

   EXAMPLES
	    rb.RemRtgDevice(device);
rtg.library/RemRtgDriver                              rtg.library/RemRtgDriver

   NAME
	RemRtgDriver -- Takes a driver off the library's list and frees it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RemRtgDriver(rb: *RtgBase, driver: *rtg.RtgDriver) bool

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -336.

   INPUTS
	- `driver` - the driver's node, as `AddRtgDriver` answered it.

   RESULT
	True if it came off. False while it has a device, or a board or a bus
	it made is still alive, and for a node that is not on the list.

   BEHAVIOR
	A driver whose boards are still alive keeps its code in use, so it is
	not taken off under them: its boards are deleted (`DeleteBoard`) and
	its devices taken away (`RemRtgDevice`) first, which only a test's
	driver sees done. Whether the node is on the list is asked
	first, by its address alone: one that is not is not read. The node and
	the driver's state behind it are freed.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, while another task holds the driver list.
	- Interrupts: no. It may wait.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held: it waits for a lock.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The node and the state are gone; the description is its module's, as
	it was.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddRtgDriver`, `DeleteBoard`, `RemRtgDevice`, `CloseTransport`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (rb.RemRtgDriver(driver)) unload();
rtg.library/RxParam                                        rtg.library/RxParam

   NAME
	RxParam -- Sends a command and reads bytes back from the part on a bus.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RxParam(_: *RtgBase, io: *rtg.RtgTransport, cmd: i32, buffer: ?*anyopaque, size: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -352.

   INPUTS
	- `io` - the transport.
	- `cmd` - the command.
	- `buffer` - where the answer goes.
	- `size` - how many bytes to read.

   RESULT
	`RTGERR_OK`, `RTGERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`, or what the driver answered.

   BEHAVIOR
	Nothing else reaches the bus between the command and the answer.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: only if the driver does.
	- Interrupts: no. The driver may wait on its bus.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held: a driver may wait.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing is allocated. The buffer is the caller's.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`TxParam`

   EXAMPLES
	    var id: [3]u8 = undefined;
	    _ = rb.RxParam(io, 0x04, &id, id.len);
rtg.library/TxColor                                        rtg.library/TxColor

   NAME
	TxColor -- Sends a command and a run of pixels to the part on a bus.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn TxColor(_: *RtgBase, io: *rtg.RtgTransport, cmd: i32, color: ?*const anyopaque, size: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -348.

   INPUTS
	- `io` - the transport.
	- `cmd` - the command.
	- `color` - the pixel bytes.
	- `size` - how many.

   RESULT
	`RTGERR_OK`, `RTGERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`, or what the driver answered -
	`RTGERR_UNDERRUN` among them: every byte went out, but some of them
	were not the ones given.

   BEHAVIOR
	It comes back when the bytes have gone: the bus has been waited for.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the bus.
	- Interrupts: no. The driver may wait on its bus.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held: a driver may wait.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The bytes stay the caller's, and are no longer read when this returns.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`TxParam`, `RxParam`

   EXAMPLES
	    _ = rb.TxColor(io, 0x2C, row.ptr, row.len);
rtg.library/TxParam                                        rtg.library/TxParam

   NAME
	TxParam -- Sends a command and its parameters to the part on a bus.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn TxParam(_: *RtgBase, io: *rtg.RtgTransport, cmd: i32, param: ?*const anyopaque, size: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -344.

   INPUTS
	- `io` - the transport.
	- `cmd` - the command; below zero sends the parameters alone.
	- `param` - the parameter bytes, or null.
	- `size` - how many.

   RESULT
	`RTGERR_OK`, `RTGERR_NOT_SUPPORTED` for a transport that cannot send,
	or what the driver answered: `RTGERR_IO`, `RTGERR_TIMEOUT`.

   BEHAVIOR
	The driver's own send, handed straight through.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: only if the driver does.
	- Interrupts: no. The driver may wait on its bus.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held: a driver may wait.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing is allocated. The bytes stay the caller's.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`TxColor`, `RxParam`

   EXAMPLES
	    _ = rb.TxParam(io, 0x29, null, 0);
